"digon-free intersecting arrangements equals ⌈4n/3⌉, see [22]. These results yield bounds for the connectivity of the respective flip graphs. (https://page.math.tu-berlin.de/~felsner/Paper/pseudo-flips.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.0 years ago; For *digon-free* arrangements the analog of Question 1 was stated as a conjecture in [23]: ... The question whether the triangle flip graph of *digon-free* intersecting cylindrical arrangements is connected remains open.\ndigon-free intersecting arrangements equals ⌈4n/3⌉, see [22]. These results yield bounds for the connectivity of the respective flip graphs.\n\nFor *digon-free* arrangements the analog of Question 1 was stated as a conjecture in [23]:\n\n**Question 6.** *Is it true that, for every n ∈ N, the triangle flip graph of digon-free intersecting arrangements of n pseudocircles is connected?*\n\nFelsner and Scheucher [23] showed that the corresponding result for digon-free intersecting arrangements of circles is true. The idea is as follows: a given arrangement of circles is projected to a unit-sphere S and the circles are extended to planes (each circle is the intersection of a plane with S). The planes are simultaneously moved towards the origin such that all circles eventually become great-circles. The connectivity then follows from the flip connectivity of great-circle arrangements. When moving the planes towards the origin, it is crucial that each Krupp remains a Krupp and each NonKrupp is eventually transformed into a Krupp; therefore no new digons appear.\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nFigure 14: Cylindrical arrangement 𝒩₆². Several colored pseudocircle curves form an arrangement around a central shaded gray triangle; each triangle (shaded gray) is formed by a Krupp, and the center is marked by ×.\n</visual_element>\n\nWe remark that a corresponding approach to transform a digon-free arrangement of pairwise intersecting pseudocircles into an arrangement of great-pseudocircles by iteratively flipping NonKrupp triangles to increase the number of triples which form a Krupp does not seem to work. The arrangement 𝒩₆² shown in Figure 14 is digon-free, contains a NonKrupp subarrangement (e.g., formed by the red, blue and black pseudocircles), but every triple of pseudocircles, which bound a triangle, form a Krupp. Note that 𝒩₆² is non-circularizable [23, Section 6.2]. By the connectivity result for digon-free intersecting circle arrangements an analogous situation cannot occur in circularizable arrangements.\n\nWith our Theorem 4 we have shown that the triangle flip graph of intersecting cylindrical arrangements of n pseudocircles is connected. The question whether the triangle flip graph of *digon-free* intersecting cylindrical arrangements is connected remains open.\n\nThe progress in the study of triangle flip graphs made in this paper may foster the future study of questions like Hamiltonicity, degree of connectivity, expansion properties, and others for classes of triangle flip graphs.\n\nOur focus in this paper was on arrangements of pseudolines and pseudocircles. Other authors have also studied other arrangements of curves with restricted intersection patterns, see e.g. [18, 12, 11]. It seems, however, that flip graphs have not been studied for families of curves beyond pseudolines and pseudocircles. Indeed one of the main tools in our studies, the sweeping lemma is not available in general. Snoeyink and Hershberger [46] describe arrangements of curves with at most 3 and 4 pairwise intersections, respectively, which cannot be swept.\n\n# References\n\n[1] P. K. Agarwal, E. Nevo, J. Pach, R. Pinchasi, M. Sharir, and S. Smorodinsky, *Lenses in arrangements of pseudo-circles and their applications*, Journal of the ACM, 51 (2004), pp. 139–186.\n\n22--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFull article: Solution to a conjecture on edge rings with 2-linear resolutions (https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00927872.2022.2137175)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.0 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; We now come to the main result, the characterization of edge rings with 2-linear resolution for which the conjecture holds. ... If k[G] is a Cohen-Macaulay ring with 2-linear resolution, then the conjecture holds for k[G].\n## 3 Main result\n\nWe now come to the main result, the characterization of edge rings with 2-linear resolution for which the conjecture holds. We first state it in terms of Stanley-Reisner rings k[\\Delta ]. If Δ is a simplex, the conjecture trivially holds. Thus we may suppose that Δ has at least two facets. It was proved in [1, Theorem 4.10] that pd (k[G)])\\ge {\\mathrm{max}}_{1\\le i\\le n}\\{{\\mathrm{deg}}_{G}({x}_{i})\\}. We will characterize those graphs for which there is equality, i.e. for which graphs the conjecture holds. An alternative proof of the inequality will also follow.\n\nTheorem 2.\n\nSuppose Δ is a quasi-forest with at least two facets, in particular k[\\Delta ] has a 2-linear resolution. Let r=\\mathrm{min}\\left\\{{r}_{i}\\right\\}. Then the conjecture holds if and only if there is a free vertex v in a facet F of Δ, \\mathrm{dim}F=r+1, F connected to the remaining part of Δ in a simplex of dimension r.\n\nProof.\n\nIf k[G]=k[\\Delta ], then two vertices in G are neighbors if and only if they are not neighbors in Δ. Thus {\\mathrm{max}}_{1\\le i\\le n}\\{{\\mathrm{deg}}_{G}({x}_{i})\\}=n-{\\mathrm{min}}_{1\\le i\\le n}\\{{\\mathrm{deg}}_{\\Delta }({x}_{i})\\}. If a vertex u in Δ lies in the facets {F}_{{i}_{1}},\\dots ,{F}_{{i}_{k}}, then all vertices in these facets are neighbors of u. A facet F has \\mathrm{dim}F+1 vertices. Now pd (k[G])=n-r-2 according to Theorem 1. We have \\mathrm{deg}v\\ge \\{{\\mathrm{dim}}_{v\\in {F}_{i}}{F}_{i}+1\\}\\ge r+2 with equality if and only v lies in only one facet F (v is free), and this facet has dimension r + 1. F is attached in the remaining part in a simplex of dimension s. We have s<\\mathrm{dim}F=r+1, and s\\ge r=\\mathrm{min}\\left\\{{r}_{i}\\right\\}, so s = r. □\n\nCorollary 1.\n\nLet G be a graph and suppose that k[G] has a 2-linear resolution. Then the conjecture holds for G if and only \\overline{G} is the 1-skeleton of a simplicial complex as in Theorem 2.\n\nCorollary 2.\n\nThe smallest counterexample to the conjecture is the edge ring of a 4-cycle, noted already in [ Citation9]. This corresponds to the Stanley-Reisner ring on four vertices \\{1,2,3,4\\} with facets \\{1,2\\},\\{3,4\\}.\n\nProof.\n\nIf G = C_{4}, then \\Delta (\\overline{G}) is the complex F on four vertices \\{1,2,3,4\\} with facets \\{1,2\\},\\{3,4\\}. We have pd (k[{C}_{4}])= pd (k[F])=4-(-1)-2=3 and \\mathrm{max}\\mathrm{deg}\\{v;v\\in G\\}=2. □\n\nCorollary 3.\n\nIf the quasi-forest Δ has an isolated vertex v, then the conjecture holds.\n\nProof.\n\nWe have \\mathrm{dim}\\left\\{v\\right\\}=0 and r = – 1. □\n\nRemark 1.\n\nThis is equivalent to [1, Theorem 4.11], which considers the case when there is a vertex in the graph with all other vertices as neighbors (a full vertex).\n\nTheorem 3.\n\n[1, Theorem 4.21] Let G be a graph such that \\overline{G} is a ({d}_{1},{d}_{2},\\dots ,{d}_{q})-tree. Then the conjecture holds.\n\nProof.\n\nThis follows from Corollary 1. □\n\nCorollary 4.\n\nIf k[G] is a Cohen-Macaulay ring with 2-linear resolution, then the conjecture holds for k[G].\n\nProof.\n\nIf k[G] is Cohen-Macaulay, then k[G]=k[\\Delta ], and the complement of the 1-skeleton of Δ is a (d,d,\\dots ,d)-tree for some d by definition of ({d}_{1},\\dots ,{d}_{q})-trees and by Theorem 1. Thus the result follows from Theorem 3. □\n\nRemark 2.\n\nThe authors of [Citation1] also give an example to show that difference pd (k[G])-\\mathrm{max}\\mathrm{deg}\\{v;v\\in G\\} can be arbirarily large. The example is the complement of the r-barbell, i.e., the complement to the graph consisting of two complete graphs K_{r} with a bridge. We give a slightly smaller example.\n\nCorollary 5.\n\nLet {K}_{r,r} be the complete bipartite graph. Then pd (k[{K}_{r,r}])-\\mathrm{max}\\mathrm{deg}\\{v;v\\in {K}_{r,r}\\}=r.\n\nProof.\n\n{K}_{r,r} is the complement to the 1-skeleton of the disjoint union {S}_{r-1}\\bigsqcup {S}_{r-1} of two (r-1)-simplices {S}_{r-1}. Now the edge ring k[{K}_{r,r}] equals the Stanley-reisner ring k[{S}_{r-1}\\bigsqcup {S}_{r-1}] and pd (k[{S}_{r-1}\\bigsqcup {S}_{r-1}])-\\mathrm{max}\\mathrm{deg}\\{v;v\\in {K}_{r,r}\\}=2r-(-1)-2-r=r-1. □\n\nRemark 3.\n\nIn [Citation1] it is claimed that the conjecture is stated in [Citation4]. This seems to be a mistake, the conjecture is not mentioned in that paper, and the name of the conjecture is a bit of the mystery.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEdge rings with $q$-linear resolutions (https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.02854)\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.1 years ago; In the present paper, we give a complete classification of connected simple graphs whose edge rings have a $q$-linear resolution with $q \\geq 2$. In particular, we show that the edge ring of a finite connected simple graph with a $q$-linear resolution, where $q \\geq 3$, is a hypersurface, which was conjectured by Hibi, Matsuda, and Tsuchiya.\nTitle: Edge rings with $q$-linear resolutions\nAuthors: Kenta Mori, Hidefumi Ohsugi, Akiyoshi Tsuchiya\nDate: Tue Oct  6 16:23:08 2020\n\nIn the present paper, we give a complete classification of connected simple graphs whose edge rings have a $q$-linear resolution with $q \\geq 2$. In particular, we show that the edge ring of a finite connected simple graph with a $q$-linear resolution, where $q \\geq 3$, is a hypersurface, which was conjectured by Hibi, Matsuda, and Tsuchiya.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSolution to a conjecture on edge rings with 2-linear resolutions (https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/pdf/10.1080/00927872.2022.2137175)\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Solution to a conjecture on edge rings with 2-linear resolutions ... An edge ring k[G] has a 2-linear resolution if and only if the complement graph G is chordal, [5, Theorem\nCOMMUNICATIONS IN ALGEBRA®\n2023, VOL. 51, NO. 4, 1447–1450\nhttps://doi.org/10.1080/00927872.2022.2137175\nSolution to a conjecture on edge rings with 2-linear resolutions\nRalf Fröberg\nDepartment of mathematics, Stockholm University, Stockholm, Sweden\nABSTRACT\nFor a graph G = (V, E) the edge ring k[G] is k[x1, . . . , xn]/I(G), where n =\n|V| and I(G) is generated by {xixj; {i, j} ∈E}. The conjecture we treat is the\nfollowing.\nARTICLE HISTORY\nReceived 14 June 2022\nRevised 25 August 2022\nCommunicated by Daniel\nErman\nConjecture 1. If k[G] has a 2-linear resolution, then the projective dimension of\nK[G], pd(k[G]), equals the maximal degree of a vertex in G.\nKEYWORDS\n2-linear resolution; edge\nring; Stanley-Reisner ring\n2020 MATHEMATICS\nSUBJECT CLASSIFICATION\n13D40\nAs far as we know, this conjecture is first mentioned in a paper by Gitler and\nValencia [7, Conjecture 4.13], and there it is called the Eliahou-Villarreal conjec-\nture. The conjecture is treated in a recent paper by Ahmed, Mafi, and Namiq\n[1]. That there are counterexamples was noted already by Moradi and Kiani [9].\nBy interpreting k[G] as a Stanley-Reisner ring, we are able to characterize those\ngraphs for which the conjecture holds.\n1. Introduction\nAn edge ring k[G] has a 2-linear resolution if and only if the complement graph G is chordal, [5, Theorem\n1], i.e., every cycle of length ≥3 in G has a chord. (The complement graph G has the same vertex set as\nG, and {i, j} is an edge in G if and only if {i, j} is not an edge in G.) There are other proofs of this theorem\nin [3, Theorem 2.1], [2, Theorem 3.4], [10, Corollary 3.3], and [8, Theorem 9.2.12].\nHowever, we prefer to use the characterization of edge rings with 2-linear resolution in terms of\nStanley-Reisner rings. The Stanley-Reisner ring of a simplicial complex \u0002 with vertex set {1, 2, . . . , n}\nis k[x1, . . . , xn]/I(\u0002), where I(\u0002) is generated by those squarefree monomials xi1 · · · xik for which\n{i1, . . . , ik} is not a face in \u0002. Then k[\u0002] has a 2-linear resolution if and only if \u0002 is a quasi-forest, [5, 6].\nQuasi-forests are also named fat forests or generalized d-trees, but we have chosen the name quasi-forest\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSolution to a conjecture on edge rings with 2-linear resolutions (https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.14436)\nciteturn1academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.4 years ago; Title: Solution to a conjecture on edge rings with 2-linear resolutions ... If $k[G]$ has a 2-linear resolution, then the projective dimension of $K[G]$, pd$(k[G])$, equals the maximal degree of a vertex in $G$. ... By interpreting $k[G]$ as a Stanley-Reisner ring, we are able to characterize those graphs for which the conjecture holds.\nTitle: Solution to a conjecture on edge rings with 2-linear resolutions\nAuthors: Ralf Fröberg\nDate: Sat May 28 13:55:07 2022\n\nFor a graph $G=(V,E)$ the edge ring $k[G]$ is $k[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]/I(G)$, where $n=|V|$ and $I(G)$ is generated by $\\{ x_ix_j;\\{ i,j\\}\\in E\\}$. The conjecture we treat is the following. If $k[G]$ has a 2-linear resolution, then the projective dimension of $K[G]$, pd$(k[G])$, equals the maximal degree of a vertex in $G$. As far as we know, this conjecture is first mentioned in a paper by Gitler and Valencia, and there it is called the Eliahou-Villarreal conjecture. The conjecture is treated in a recent paper by Ahmed, Mafi, and Namiq. That there are counterexamples was noted already by Moradi and Kiani. By interpreting $k[G]$ as a Stanley-Reisner ring, we are able to characterize those graphs for which the conjecture holds.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArrangements: Combinatorial and Computational Problems (https://page.math.tu-berlin.de/~roch/files/diss_draft.pdf)\nciteturn1search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; In Chapter 3, we present a result from [FRS24] that confirms the conjecture in the special case in which there are three pseudocircles that pairwise form a digon. ... For digon-free arrangements, a lower bound of ⌈4/3 n⌉ triangles was proven in [SH91], and in Chapter 3, we present a result from [FRS24] showing that this bound is tight. ... We show that, similarly to the case of pseudoline arrangements, the triangle flip graph of intersecting pseudocircle arrangements is connected.\nIt turns out that his first conjecture is correct. In Chapter 3, we present a result from [FRS24] that confirms the conjecture in the special case in which there are three pseudocircles that pairwise form a digon. We also construct a new infinite family of arrangements with 2n − 2 digons. In the meantime, the conjecture was proven in general [ADK+25a]. The second conjecture, however, has been disproven in [FS21]. For digon-free arrangements, a lower bound of ⌈4/3 n⌉ triangles was proven in [SH91], and in Chapter 3, we present a result from [FRS24] showing that this bound is tight.\n\nOur next contribution is from [ARFO+24]. We show that, similarly to the case of pseudoline arrangements, the triangle flip graph of intersecting pseudocircle arrangements is connected. First, we show that the subclass of cylindrical arrangements is connected via triangle flips, and next, that every intersecting pseudocircle arrangement can be made cylindrical by triangle flips. The property cylindrical means that there exists a point contained in the interior regions of all pseudocircles. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSergi Elizalde | ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/author/6507360243/sergi-elizalde)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Moore ... In this paper, we give a complete characterization of the permutations (also called patterns) realized by signed shifts. In the case of the negative shift, which is the signed shift having only negative slopes, we use our characterization to give an exact enumeration of these patterns. ... Finally, we show that the monotone pattern asymptotically dominates (in the sense that it is easiest to avoid) all non-overlapping patterns of the same length, thus proving a conjecture of Elizalde and Noy for a positive fraction of all patterns.\n\n  * Research article Open archive\n\nCharacterizations and enumerations of patterns of signed shifts\n\nS. Elizalde, K. Moore\n\nDiscrete Applied Mathematics • Volume 277 • 30 April 2020\n\n    * View PDF\n    * View abstract\n\nShift maps can be interpreted as maps from the unit interval to itself sending to the fractional part of , whose graph consists of segments of positive slope. Signed shifts generalize shift maps by allowing some of these segments to have negative slope. Permutations realized by the relative order of the elements in the orbits of these maps have been studied recently by Amigó, Archer and Elizalde. In this paper, we give a complete characterization of the permutations (also called patterns) realized by signed shifts. In the case of the negative shift, which is the signed shift having only negative slopes, we use our characterization to give an exact enumeration of these patterns. Finally, we improve the best known bounds for the number of patterns realized by the tent map, and calculate the topological entropy of signed shifts using these combinatorial methods.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEDGE RINGS WITH q-LINEAR RESOLUTIONS (https://static.aminer.cn/storage/pdf/arxiv/20/2010/2010.02854.pdf)\nciteturn1search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; EDGE RINGS WITH q-LINEAR RESOLUTIONS ... a q-linear resolution, where q ≥4, is a hypersurface ([9, Conjecture 0.2]). ... Key words and phrases. ﬁnite graph, edge ring, linear resolution, δ-polynomial.\nEDGE RINGS WITH q-LINEAR RESOLUTIONS\nKENTA MORI, HIDEFUMI OHSUGI AND AKIYOSHI TSUCHIYA\nABSTRACT. In the present paper, we give a complete classiﬁcation of connected simple\ngraphs whose edge rings have a q-linear resolution with q ≥2. In particular, we show that\nthe edge ring of a ﬁnite connected simple graph with a q-linear resolution, where q ≥3,\nis a hypersurface, which was conjectured by Hibi, Matsuda, and Tsuchiya.\n1. INTRODUCTION\nLet K be a ﬁeld and K[x] := K[x1,...,xn] the polynomial ring with n variables over K.\nFor a ﬁnite simple graph G on the vertex set [n] := {1,...,n}, the edge ring K[G] of G\nis the K-subalgebra of K[x] generated by the quadratic monomials xixj corresponding to\nthe edges {i, j} of G. Recently, edge rings and the associated lattice polytopes, which\nare called edge polytopes, have been studied from the viewpoints of combinatorics, graph\ntheory, geometric algebra, and commutative algebra. In particular, there has been signif-\nicant interest in understanding the minimal free resolutions of K[G] for several classes of\ngraphs [2, 6, 9, 10, 11, 15, 16, 18]. We are interested in which edge ring has a q-linear\nresolution, where q ≥2. Previously, in [15], Hibi and the second author gave an alge-\nbraic characterization of ﬁnite connected simple graphs whose edge rings have 2-linear\nresolutions.\nProposition 1.1 ([15, Theorem 4.6]). Let G be a ﬁnite connected simple graph on [n].\nThen K[G] has a 2-linear resolution if and only if K[G] is isomorphic to the polynomial\nring in n−2δ variables over the edge ring K[K2,δ] of the complete bipartite graph K2,δ.\narXiv:2010.02854v1  [math.AC]  6 Oct 2020\nFurthermore, in [9], Hibi, Matsuda, and the third author showed an algebraic property\nof ﬁnite connected simple graphs whose edge rings have 3-linear resolutions.\nProposition 1.2 ([9, Theorem 0.1]). The edge ring of a ﬁnite connected simple graph with\na 3-linear resolution is a hypersurface.\nIn addition, they conjectured that the edge ring of a ﬁnite connected simple graph with\na q-linear resolution, where q ≥4, is a hypersurface ([9, Conjecture 0.2]). Recently, this\nconjecture has been proved for the cases of chordal graphs [11] and bipartite graphs [18].\nIn the present paper, we prove this conjecture for any ﬁnite connected simple graph.\nTheorem 1.3. The edge ring of a ﬁnite connected simple graph with a q-linear resolution,\nwhere q ≥4, is a hypersurface.\n2010 Mathematics Subject Classiﬁcation. 05E40; 13H10; 52B20.\nKey words and phrases. ﬁnite graph, edge ring, linear resolution, δ-polynomial.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEdge rings with $3$-linear resolutions (https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.03504)\nciteturn1academia18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.9 years ago; Title: Edge rings with $3$-linear resolutions ... It is shown that the edge ring of a finite connected simple graph with a $3$-linear resolution is a hypersurface.\nTitle: Edge rings with $3$-linear resolutions\nAuthors: Takayuki Hibi, Kazunori Matsuda, Akiyoshi Tsuchiya\nDate: Sun Dec 10 11:04:25 2017\n\nIt is shown that the edge ring of a finite connected simple graph with a $3$-linear resolution is a hypersurface.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEdge rings of bipartite graphs with linear resolutions (https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.05678)\nciteturn1academia19 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.2 years ago; Title: Edge rings of bipartite graphs with linear resolutions ... On the other hand, Hibi, Matsuda and the author conjectured that the edge ring of a finite connected simple graph with a $q$-linear resolution, where $q \\geq 3$, is a hypersurface and proved the case $q=3$. In the present paper, we solve this conjecture for the case of finite connected simple bipartite graphs.\nTitle: Edge rings of bipartite graphs with linear resolutions\nAuthors: Akiyoshi Tsuchiya\nDate: Thu Aug 15 13:21:13 2019\n\nOhsugi and Hibi characterized the edge ring of a finite connected simple graph with a $2$-linear resolution. On the other hand, Hibi, Matsuda and the author conjectured that the edge ring of a finite connected simple graph with a $q$-linear resolution, where $q \\geq 3$, is a hypersurface and proved the case $q=3$. In the present paper, we solve this conjecture for the case of finite connected simple bipartite graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRoot polytopes, tropical types, and toric edge ideals (https://alco.centre-mersenne.org/item/10.5802/alco.404.pdf)\nciteturn1search20 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; We next use our tools to investigate toric edge rings of bipartite graphs that have linear resolutions (see Definition 6.2). ... In [28] Hibi, Matsuda, and Tsuchiya prove that if \\(\\Bbbk[G]\\) is the edge ring of a finite connected simple graph with a 3-linear resolution then \\(\\Bbbk[G]\\) is a hypersurface. Moreover they conjecture that the same is true for any\nHowever, \\(B\\) has a maximal matching of size \\(n\\), and hence the bound in Corollary 6.7 is as bad as possible.\n\nExample 6.16. Let \\(B\\) be the 4-cycles \\(C_1,\\ldots,C_k\\) with an edge between \\(C_i\\) and \\(C_{i+1}\\) for each \\(1 \\le i \\le k\\), as shown in Figure 10. Taking two disjoint edges in each \\(C_i\\) gives a maximal matching of size \\(2k\\), however the recession connectivity of \\(B\\) is \\(k+1\\). This is as any subgraph \\(S\\) such that \\(R(S;B)\\) is strongly connected must contain all edges between cycles; Figure 10 gives an example of such a subgraph.\n\nWe note that adding a single edge to a graph can have a huge impact on the regularity of the edge ring. Consider the bipartite graph \\(B'\\) obtained by adding a single edge to \\(B\\) between the cycles \\(C_1\\) and \\(C_k\\), as depicted in Figure 10. \\(B'\\) has the same matching number as \\